---

<BaseLayout
  title="Birmingham PHV Test — Frequently Asked Questions"
  description="Birmingham PHV test FAQs: how many questions, the auto-fail rules, why there is no pass mark, the DBS, medical and driving test, and how to prepare."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <main class="md:flex md:justify-center">
    <article class="prose prose-lg max-w-[750px]">
      <h1>Birmingham PHV Test — Frequently Asked Questions</h1>

      <p>
        Quick answers to the questions drivers ask most about the
        <strong>Birmingham PHV test</strong> and the licensing process behind
        it. For the full picture, see our guides on
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/birmingham/how-to-pass/"
          >passing the Birmingham PHV test</a
        >
        and
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/birmingham/how-to-apply/"
          >applying for a Birmingham PHV licence</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2>About the test</h2>

      <h3>How many questions are on the Birmingham taxi badge test?</h3>

      <p>
        <strong>18 questions</strong>, in three sections of 6 — Legal, Licence
        Conditions and Plying for Hire — with <strong>45 minutes</strong>
        allowed.
      </p>

      <h3>What are the auto-fail rules?</h3>

      <p>
        <strong>Every Plying for Hire question must be correct.</strong> One
        wrong answer in that section fails the paper on its own, whatever you
        scored elsewhere.
      </p>

      <p>
        On top of that, you may drop <strong>no more than two in Legal</strong>,
        <strong>no more than two in Licence Conditions</strong>, and
        <strong>no more than four across those two sections combined</strong>.
      </p>

      <h3>Is there a pass mark?</h3>

      <p>
        No — and this is the part most people get wrong. Birmingham does not
        use an overall pass mark at all. The paper is judged section by
        section, so a high total is no guarantee of a pass.
      </p>

      <h3>Can I really fail with 17 out of 18?</h3>

      <p>
        Yes, if the question you dropped was a Plying for Hire one. That is the
        whole point of an auto-fail section: nothing else on the paper can
        compensate for it.
      </p>

      <h3>Who runs the test?</h3>

      <p>
        <strong>TTC Group</strong>, not the council. Birmingham City Council
        states that private hire knowledge tests are organised and conducted by
        TTC, who also offer an optional preparation course and a short video on
        how to prepare. You book by contacting TTC directly by email.
      </p>

      <h3>Is it the same test as Wolverhampton or Solihull?</h3>

      <p>
        No. Birmingham is 18 questions with section rules and no pass mark;
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/how-to-pass/">Wolverhampton</a> is 28 with
        8 key questions and a 21/28 mark;
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/solihull/how-to-pass/">Solihull</a> is 48
        with a 36/48 mark and no auto-fail. A pass with one council does not
        qualify you with another.
      </p>

      <h2>About applying</h2>

      <h3>Is Birmingham accepting new driver applications?</h3>

      <p>
        The council's applications page carries a notice saying new driver
        applications are not being accepted unless you have already completed a
        medical and obtained a DBS certificate through its occupational health
        service. Newer guidance pages describe the full route, so the two do
        not read consistently — confirm the current position with
        <a href="mailto:licensing@birmingham.gov.uk">licensing@birmingham.gov.uk</a>
        before spending money.
      </p>

      <h3>What checks do I need?</h3>

      <p>
        A <strong>medical</strong>, booked and paid for through the council's
        online form; a <strong>conviction disclosures check (DBS)</strong>, or
        proof of previous good character by other means as set out in the
        driver guidance; and a <strong>driving test</strong> — new applicants
        are required to take one with the Birmingham City Council Driving
        School.
      </p>

      <h3>How do I book the driving test?</h3>

      <p>
        Phone <strong>0121 303 3311</strong> between 9:00am and 12:00pm or
        1:00pm and 3:00pm. You may reach an answerphone, as assessors are often
        out on jobs.
      </p>

      <h3>When do I fill in the application form?</h3>

      <p>
        <strong>After you have passed the knowledge test.</strong> You then
        download and complete the form and make an appointment to bring it in
        with the documents listed in the council's Guide to Driver Licences.
      </p>

      <h2>About the app</h2>

      <h3>Are the practice questions specific to Birmingham?</h3>

      <p>
        Yes. Every question in the app's Birmingham mode is built from official
        Birmingham City Council source material — council policy documents,
        licensing conditions and relevant UK legislation. This isn't generic
        PHV content.
      </p>

      <h3>Will mock exams just repeat the same questions?</h3>

      <p>
        No. Each mock exam pulls 18 questions — 6 from each of the three
        sections — from the full <strong>150+</strong> question bank, so you
        get a different paper every time. All questions are distinct: no
        padding or rephrased duplicates.
      </p>

      <h3>Does the app mark papers the way Birmingham does?</h3>

      <p>
        Yes. It applies the section rules rather than a score, so a mock that
        would have failed on Plying for Hire is reported as a fail — not as
        "17 out of 18".
      </p>

      <h3>Is this useful if I've already failed the test?</h3>

      <p>
        Especially then. The most common way to fail is Plying for Hire: every
        question in that section must be correct, so one wrong answer fails the
        paper even with everything else right, and there is no overall pass
        mark to make up for it. The app shows your score section by section, so
        you can see whether you are losing the paper there or drifting over the
        two-wrong limit elsewhere.
      </p>

      <h3>Do you have pass rate statistics?</h3>

      <p>
        No, and we won't invent one. We can't verify what happens after someone
        leaves the app and sits the real test. Anyone quoting you a percentage
        for something like this is guessing.
      </p>

      <p>
